// Implement the decode function to extract information from the input byte array
pub fn decode<'a>(data: &'a [u8]) -> (EventType, u32, &'a [u8], &'a [u8]) {
    // Extract the event type from the first byte of the input byte array
    let event_type = match data.get(0) {
        Some(&1) => EventType::Type1,
        Some(&2) => EventType::Type2,
        Some(&3) => EventType::Type3,
        _ => panic!("Unknown event type"),
    };

    // Extract the unsigned 32-bit integer from the next 4 bytes of the input byte array
    let id = u32::from_le_bytes(data[1..5].try_into().unwrap());

    // Find the index of the zero byte to split the byte array
    let zero_index = data.iter().position(|&x| x == 0).unwrap();

    // Extract the first variable-length byte array before the zero byte
    let first_array = &data[5..zero_index];

    // Extract the second variable-length byte array after the zero byte
    let second_array = &data[zero_index + 1..];

    (event_type, id, first_array, second_array)
}
```

In the solution, we first define the `EventType` enum to represent different event types. Then, we implement the `decode` function to extract the event type, ID, and two variable-length byte arrays from the input byte array. The function uses pattern matching to determine the event type, extracts the ID using little-endian byte order, and splits the input byte array to extract the two variable-length byte arrays.

