impl Lexer {
    pub fn parse_string(&mut self) -> JsResult<bool> {
        let mut parsed_successfully = true;
        let mut string_literal = String::new();

        loop {
            let escaped = self.is_escaped();
            let c = self.current_char();
            let t = match self.mode() {
                LexerMode::String(t) => t,
                _ => {
                    panic!("Unhandled Parser State Reached: {:?}, {:?}, {:?}, col {:?}, line {:?}", c, self.mode(), self.is_escaped(), self.col(), self.line())
                }
            };

            if c == '"' && !escaped {
                // End of string literal
                break;
            } else if c == '\\' && escaped {
                // Handle escape sequences
                match self.current_char() {
                    'n' => string_literal.push('\n'),
                    't' => string_literal.push('\t'),
                    '\\' => string_literal.push('\\'),
                    '"' => string_literal.push('"'),
                    _ => {
                        // Invalid escape sequence
                        parsed_successfully = false;
                        break;
                    }
                }
                self.advance(); // Move to the next character after handling escape sequence
            } else {
                string_literal.push(c);
            }

            if !self.advance() {
                // End of input reached before end of string literal
                parsed_successfully = false;
                break;
            }
        }

        if parsed_successfully {
            // Successfully parsed the string literal
            // Further processing or tokenization can be done here
            println!("Parsed string literal: {}", string_literal);
        } else {
            // Error occurred during parsing
            println!("Error parsing string literal");
        }

        Ok(parsed_successfully)
    }
}
```

In the solution, the `parse_string` method iterates through the characters of the input string, handling escape sequences and building the string literal. It returns `true` if the string is successfully parsed, and `false` if an error occurs during parsing. The method also includes error handling for invalid escape sequences and premature end of input.

