fn bury_operation(mut stack: Vec<i32>) -> Result<Vec<i32>, String> {
    if stack.len() < 3 {
        return Err(format!("'bury' requires 3 items on top of the stack, '{}' found.", stack.len()));
    } else {
        let top = stack.pop().unwrap();
        let middle = stack.pop().unwrap();
        let bottom = stack.pop().unwrap();
        stack.push(top);
        stack.push(bottom);
        stack.push(middle);
        Ok(stack)
    }
}

fn main() {
    let stack1 = vec![1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
    match bury_operation(stack1) {
        Ok(modified_stack) => println!("Modified Stack: {:?}", modified_stack),
        Err(err) => println!("Error: {}", err),
    }

    let stack2 = vec![1, 2];
    match bury_operation(stack2) {
        Ok(modified_stack) => println!("Modified Stack: {:?}", modified_stack),
        Err(err) => println!("Error: {}", err),
    }
}
```

The `bury_operation` function takes a stack as input and performs the "bury" operation. If the stack has less than 3 items, it returns an error message. Otherwise, it rearranges the top three items on the stack in reverse order and returns the modified stack. The `main` function demonstrates the usage of the `bury_operation` function with example stacks and handles the returned result accordingly.

