```rust
fn build_arp_msg_ipv4(
    ptype: EtherType,
    operation: ARPOperation,
    sha: MACAddr,
    spa: IPV4Addr,
    tha: MACAddr,
    tpa: IPV4Addr,
) -> [u8; 28] {
    let mut arp_msg: [u8; 28] = [0; 28];

    // Set hardware type (Ethernet) - 2 bytes
    arp_msg[0] = 0x00;
    arp_msg[1] = ptype as u8;

    // Set protocol type (IPv4) - 2 bytes
    arp_msg[2] = 0x08;
    arp_msg[3] = 0x00;

    // Set hardware address length - 1 byte
    arp_msg[4] = 6;

    // Set protocol address length - 1 byte
    arp_msg[5] = 4;

    // Set operation - 2 bytes
    arp_msg[6] = 0x00;
    arp_msg[7] = operation as u8;

    // Set source hardware address - 6 bytes
    for i in 0..6 {
        arp_msg[8 + i] = sha.addr[i];
    }

    // Set source protocol address - 4 bytes
    for i in 0..4 {
        arp_msg[14 + i] = spa.addr[i];
    }

    // Set target hardware address - 6 bytes
    for i in 0..6 {
        arp_msg[18 + i] = tha.addr[i];
    }

    // Set target protocol address - 4 bytes
    for i in 0..4 {
        arp_msg[24 + i] = tpa.addr[i];
    }

    arp_msg
}
```
This solution implements the `build_arp_msg_ipv4` function to construct the ARP message for IPv4 based on the provided input fields. It initializes a byte array `arp_msg` of size 28 and sets each field of the ARP message according to the specified format. The function then returns the constructed ARP message as a byte array.

