The function should take a reference to a string `input` and an array of string references `words`. It should return a vector of distances, where each distance represents the multi-modified Damerau-Levenshtein distance between the `input` string and each string in the `words` array.

For example, given the input string "aderua" and the array of strings ["damerau"], the function should return a vector with a single distance value representing the multi-modified Damerau-Levenshtein distance between "aderua" and "damerau".

Your task is to implement the `multi_modified_damlev` function to calculate the multi-modified Damerau-Levenshtein distance for each string in the `words` array and the `input` string.

```rust
fn multi_modified_damlev(input: &str, words: &[&str]) -> Vec<usize> {
    let mut distances = Vec::new();

    for word in words {
        let distance = modified_damlev(input, word);
        distances.push(distance);
    }

    distances
}

fn modified_damlev(s1: &str, s2: &str) -> usize {
    let len1 = s1.chars().count();
    let len2 = s2.chars().count();

    let mut d: Vec<Vec<usize>> = vec![vec![0; len2 + 1]; len1 + 1];

    for i in 0..=len1 {
        d[i][0] = i;
    }
    for j in 0..=len2 {
        d[0][j] = j;
    }

    for (i, c1) in s1.chars().enumerate() {
        for (j, c2) in s2.chars().enumerate() {
            let cost = if c1 == c2 { 0 } else { 1 };
            d[i + 1][j + 1] = *[
                d[i][j + 1] + 1,
                d[i + 1][j] + 1,
                d[i][j] + cost,
            ]
            .iter()
            .min()
            .unwrap();
            if i > 0 && j > 0 && c1 != c2 && s1.chars().nth(i - 1) == s2.chars().nth(j)
                && s1.chars().nth(i) == s2.chars().nth(j - 1)
            {
                d[i + 1][j + 1] = d[i + 1][j + 1].min(d[i - 1][j - 1] + cost);
            }
        }
    }

    d[len1][len2]
}
```

This solution provides the implementation for the `multi_modified_damlev` function, which calculates the multi-modified Damerau-Levenshtein distance between the `input` string and each string in the `words` array. It also includes the helper function `modified_damlev` to calculate the Damerau-Levenshtein distance between two strings. The solution uses dynamic programming to efficiently compute the distances and returns a vector containing the distances for each word in the input array.

