#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone)]
pub enum TransactionId {
    NoTransaction,
    StartTransaction,
    CommitTransaction,
    RollbackTransaction,
}

pub struct Transaction {
    state: TransactionId,
}

impl Transaction {
    pub fn new() -> Transaction {
        Transaction { state: TransactionId::NoTransaction }
    }

    pub fn start(&mut self) {
        self.state = TransactionId::StartTransaction;
    }

    pub fn commit(&mut self) -> Result<(), &str> {
        match self.state {
            TransactionId::StartTransaction => {
                self.state = TransactionId::CommitTransaction;
                Ok(())
            }
            _ => Err("Cannot commit transaction in current state"),
        }
    }

    pub fn rollback(&mut self) -> Result<(), &str> {
        match self.state {
            TransactionId::StartTransaction => {
                self.state = TransactionId::RollbackTransaction;
                Ok(())
            }
            _ => Err("Cannot rollback transaction in current state"),
        }
    }
}

fn main() {
    let mut transaction = Transaction::new();
    transaction.start();
    println!("{:?}", transaction); // Output: Transaction { state: StartTransaction }

    let commit_result = transaction.commit();
    println!("{:?}", transaction); // Output: Transaction { state: CommitTransaction }
    println!("{:?}", commit_result); // Output: Ok(())

    let rollback_result = transaction.rollback();
    println!("{:?}", transaction); // Output: Transaction { state: RollbackTransaction }
    println!("{:?}", rollback_result); // Output: Ok(())
}
```

In this solution, the `TransactionId` enum is extended to include the `CommitTransaction` and `RollbackTransaction` variants. The `Transaction` struct is implemented with methods to start, commit, and roll back transactions, ensuring that the transaction system follows the specified rules. The `main` function demonstrates the usage of the transaction system by starting, committing, and rolling back a transaction.

