1. Efficiently store key-value pairs using the HAMT data structure.
2. Implement the `insert` method to insert a key-value pair into the HAMT.
3. Implement the `get` method to retrieve the value associated with a given key.

You can assume that the `Hamt` struct will be used to store key-value pairs where the keys are of type `u32` and the values are of type `String`.

```rust
// hamt.rs

use std::collections::HashMap;

const MAX_INDEX: usize = 32;
const MAX_BRANCHING: usize = 5;

pub struct Hamt {
    children: Option<Box<[Option<Hamt>]>>,
    values: Option<HashMap<u32, String>>,
}

impl Hamt {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        Hamt {
            children: None,
            values: None,
        }
    }

    pub fn insert(&mut self, key: u32, value: String) {
        let mut current = self;
        let mut key_bits = key;
        let mut level = 0;

        while level < MAX_INDEX {
            let index = (key_bits & (MAX_BRANCHING as u32 - 1)) as usize;
            key_bits >>= MAX_BRANCHING as u32;

            if let Some(ref mut children) = current.children {
                if let Some(ref mut child) = &mut children[index] {
                    current = child;
                } else {
                    let mut new_child = Hamt::new();
                    current = &mut new_child;
                    children[index] = Some(new_child);
                }
            } else {
                let mut children = vec![None; MAX_BRANCHING];
                let mut new_child = Hamt::new();
                current.children = Some(children.into_boxed_slice());
                current = &mut new_child;
                current.children.as_mut().unwrap()[index] = Some(new_child);
            }

            level += MAX_BRANCHING;
        }

        if current.values.is_none() {
            current.values = Some(HashMap::new());
        }

        current.values.as_mut().unwrap().insert(key, value);
    }

    pub fn get(&self, key: u32) -> Option<&String> {
        let mut current = self;
        let mut key_bits = key;
        let mut level = 0;

        while level < MAX_INDEX {
            let index = (key_bits & (MAX_BRANCHING as u32 - 1)) as usize;
            key_bits >>= MAX_BRANCHING as u32;

            if let Some(ref children) = current.children {
                if let Some(ref child) = &children[index] {
                    current = child;
                } else {
                    return None;
                }
            } else {
                return None;
            }

            level += MAX_BRANCHING;
        }

        current.values.as_ref().and_then(|values| values.get(&key))
    }
}
```

This solution provides a complete implementation of the `Hamt` data structure with the `insert` and `get` methods. The `insert` method efficiently inserts key-value pairs into the HAMT, and the `get` method retrieves the value associated with a given key. The HAMT uses a trie-like structure to store key-value pairs, providing efficient lookup and insertion operations.

