fn deserialize_hex_string(hex_string: &str) -> Result<Vec<u8>, String> {
    let mut hex_string = hex_string.trim_start_matches("0x"); // Remove optional '0x' prefix
    if hex_string.len() % 2 != 0 {
        return Err("Invalid hexadecimal string".to_string()); // Length must be even
    }

    let mut bytes = Vec::new();
    while !hex_string.is_empty() {
        let (byte, rest) = match u8::from_str_radix(&hex_string[..2], 16) {
            Ok(byte) => (byte, &hex_string[2..]),
            Err(_) => return Err("Invalid hexadecimal string".to_string()), // Invalid byte
        };
        bytes.push(byte);
        hex_string = rest;
    }

    Ok(bytes)
}
```

This solution first removes the optional '0x' prefix from the input string. It then checks if the length of the remaining string is even, as each byte in hexadecimal representation consists of two characters. If the length is not even, it returns an error. Otherwise, it iterates through the string, parsing each pair of characters as a hexadecimal byte and appending it to the result vector. If any parsing fails, it returns an error. Finally, it returns the vector of bytes if successful.

