        total_buffer_written += data_read;

        println!("Total data sent over the network: {}", total_buffer_written);
        println!("Server Response: {}", String::from_utf8(data[0..data_read].to_vec()).unwrap());

        // Simulate sending data back to the server
        let bytes_written = stream.write(&data[0..data_read]).await.unwrap();
        println!("Bytes written to stream: {}", bytes_written);
        println!("Remaining data to send: {}", data_read - bytes_written);

        // Add a delay to simulate real-time data transfer
        tokio::time::sleep(std::time::Duration::from_secs(1)).await;
    }
}
```

In this solution, we use the Tokio asynchronous runtime to create a TCP stream and simulate data transfer. We track the total amount of data sent over the network by incrementing the `total_buffer_written` variable with the amount of data read from the stream. We then provide real-time feedback on the data being transferred by printing the total data sent and the server response. Additionally, we simulate sending data back to the server and print the bytes written and remaining data to send. Finally, we introduce a delay to simulate real-time data transfer.

